<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rostam</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rustam</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rostam is the greatest hero of Iranian epic, celebrated above all in Ferdowsi's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a>, completed around 1010. Lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabulistan">Zabulistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a> and son of Zal, he rides the horse Rakhsh and defends the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> against their enemies through many generations, and his tragic killing of his son Sohrab is one of the poem's best-known episodes. Some scholars trace elements of his legend to Saka tradition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rostam.jpg" alt="Rostam" width="320" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Artists of the court of Bāysunghur in Herat. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rostam_and_the_White_Div_from_Baysanghar_Shahnameh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His young son Baysunghur was soon displaced by his cousin <b>Rustam</b> (r. 1492–1497), who in turn was overthrown by Ahmad, a grandson of Uzun Hasan who had grown up at the Ottoman court and who was killed within months after trying to curb the emirs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In Iranian tradition the hero <b>Rostam</b> of the Shahnameh is lord of Sistan, and some scholars trace his legends to Saka epic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saka">Saka · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Shahnameh, the “Book of Kings”, completed by Ferdowsi around 1010 after some thirty years of work: an epic of some 50,000 couplets telling the history of Iran from the creation of the world to the Arab conquest, with the tragedies of heroes such as <b>Rostam</b> and Sohrab.</blockquote><a href="/language/persian">Persian · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>…century into the 8th, and the halls of houses were covered with scenes of gods, banquets, hunts and mourning, with episodes from epics, among them the deeds of the hero <b>Rustam</b>, later famous from the Persian Shahnameh, and with fables also known from Aesop and the Indian Panchatantra.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sogdians">Sogdians · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Legends of <b>Rostam</b> were known across the Iranian lands by the end of the Sasanian period; Markwart traced him to Parthian origins, and his home, Sakastan, was the ancestral seat of the House of Suren, one of the seven great Parthian families.</blockquote><a href="/work/shahnameh">Shahnameh · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Abbasid officer Ahmad ibn Kayghalagh led the annual raid against the Byzantine Empire from Tarsus with the local governor, <b>Rustam</b> ibn Baradu.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-22">22 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabulistan">Zabulistan</a>. Died in Kabulistan. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
